---
# THIS deployment's management identity: NTP, VTP, and optionally SNMPv3 and
# syslog.
#
# NTP and VTP are required. SNMPv3 and syslog are ENROLLED BY PRESENCE: declare
# the keys and readiness.yml checks and configures them, comment them out and it
# ignores them completely. Each optional block below says so at its head.
#
#   cp group_vars/all/deployment.yml.example group_vars/all/deployment.yml
#   $EDITOR group_vars/all/deployment.yml
#
# Deployment-wide, not per fabric -- one estate has one NTP source and one SNMP
# identity however many fabrics it runs. Gitignored, because these are real
# addresses and names on a real network.
#
# Read only by roles/underlay_readiness, which asserts them and applies the
# platform baseline. generate_evpn.yml and deploy_evpn.yml need nothing from
# this file: run either on a fresh clone with no configuration at all.
#
# No defaults are offered for any of it. An NTP server pointing at somebody
# else's network is worse than a clean failure, so the role fails with a pointer
# to this file instead.

# Addresses use RFC 5737 documentation ranges. Replace all of them.

# --- REQUIRED: NTP ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# A LIST, shaped like snmp_acl and syslog_hosts below -- one 'ntp server' line
# per entry. This replaced ntp_server_1 / ntp_server_2, which fixed the count at
# exactly two: a deployment with three sources had nowhere to put the third, and
# one with a single source had to name it twice.
#
# Required, unlike the two optional blocks below. At least one entry, though two
# or more is strongly preferable: a single time source cannot be sanity-checked,
# and the switch will follow it wherever it goes.
#
# Bare IPv4 addresses, no masks and no hostnames.
ntp_servers:
  - "198.51.100.10"
  - "198.51.100.11"

# --- OPTIONAL: SNMPv3 -------------------------------------------------------
#
# ENROLLED BY BEING HERE. Leave all three keys below set and readiness.yml
# reads SNMPv3 state off each switch, configures the group and user, and reports
# a switch that is missing either. Comment all three out and it does none of
# those things -- no read, no push, no check, no finding. There is no flag to
# pass; a deployment that has no SNMP collector says so by not naming one.
#
# ALL THREE OR NONE. Half a set fails the run rather than quietly disabling
# SNMP, because a mistyped key name would otherwise read exactly like a
# deliberate opt-out and would silently stop configuring every switch.
#
# The passwords come from the environment, never from here -- see SETUP.md.
# Only the identity lives in this file.
snmp_v3_user: "example-snmpv3"
snmp_v3_group: "AuthPrivGroup"
snmp_acl_name: "SNMP-ACL"

# The collectors permitted to poll. OPTIONAL WITHIN the above: an enrolled
# deployment that leaves this out (or empty) gets the group with no 'access'
# clause and no ACL, which permits any source.
#
# This is the list to check first when a poll times out. A collector that is not
# named here is dropped by the switch silently -- no auth error, no report PDU,
# just no answer at all.
snmp_acl:
  - "198.51.100.20"

# --- OPTIONAL: syslog -------------------------------------------------------
#
# ENROLLED BY BEING HERE, exactly like the SNMPv3 keys above. Every collector
# listed gets a 'logging host' line, and each switch gets the trap level and
# facility that go with them:
#
#     logging trap notifications          <- syslog_severity, see below
#     logging facility syslog
#     logging host 198.51.100.30
#     logging host 198.51.100.31
#
# readiness.yml pushes those with the platform baseline and then checks all of
# them are present. Comment the whole key out -- including its list items --
# and nothing syslog-related is pushed, checked or reported.
#
# A LIST, like snmp_acl and ntp_servers, so two collectors need no second
# variable. Each entry must be a bare IPv4 address: no mask, no hostname, no
# port. Anything else fails the run rather than being quietly dropped, because
# a typo must not be able to masquerade as an opt-out.
#
# TRUE OF ALL THREE LISTS HERE: adding an entry works, and CHANGING one does
# not do what it looks like. These drive list-style IOS commands and the role
# merges, so the address you replaced stays configured on every switch until
# somebody removes it by hand -- and nothing here reports it, because every
# check tests for presence, not absence. Repoint a collector and the switches
# talk to both.
#
# 'logging source-interface Loopback0' is applied either way. It is part of the
# platform baseline's source-interface hygiene, not part of this enrolment.
syslog_hosts:
  - "198.51.100.30"
  - "198.51.100.31"

# The trap severity -- how much each switch sends to those collectors. OPTIONAL
# and meaningful only alongside syslog_hosts. Left out entirely it is
# 'notifications', which is what this role pushed when the line was a literal,
# so a deployment.yml written before this key existed behaves exactly as it did.
#
# TWO FORMS, ORDER-MATCHED. Give it a number or a name:
#
#     0 emergencies     1 alerts        2 critical        3 errors
#     4 warnings        5 notifications 6 informational   7 debugging
#
# A number is converted to its keyword before anything reaches a device, so the
# switch only ever sees 'logging trap <keyword>'. Anything that is neither one
# of those eight names nor a number in 0-7 fails the run rather than falling
# back to a default -- the same rule the address lists follow, and for the same
# reason: a typo must not be able to look like a decision.
#
# ALL EIGHT VALUES WORK, including 'informational', and that took a fix rather
# than luck. MEASURED on a C9300, a C9400 and a c9kv on 2026-08-22: IOS-XE omits
# 'logging trap' from 'show running-config' entirely when it matches the
# platform DEFAULT, which informational is. The command is in force -- 'show
# logging' says so -- but the line is not there to be read back.
#
# So this one line is NOT checked against the running config and is not part of
# the platform baseline. It is read from 'show logging', compared against what
# you asked for, and pushed only when the two differ -- the same treatment
# 'snmp-server user' gets, and for the same reason. Nothing here needs a second
# run to settle.
#
# Severity 6 and 7 are where the volume is, and the collector is what has to
# hold it. Before raising it fabric-wide, note that 'logging buffered' already
# runs at level debugging: severity 6 and 7 are ON THE SWITCH either way,
# reachable with 'show logging', and this key only decides whether they also
# cross the network. If you want them from one switch during one test, read
# that switch's buffer rather than raising the trap on eleven.
syslog_severity: notifications

# VTP domain. Every switch runs transparent mode, so this is cosmetic; the
# password and version are deliberately not managed at all (see the role
# README -- IOS-XE never reports them back, so they could never converge).
vtp_domain: "EXAMPLE"
